        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Bell Beaker culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Western and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Ireland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and parts of north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>; key sites the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a> estuary, Ross Island, Pömmelte, Stonehenge</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">From c. 2800 BCE (the earliest maritime beakers, in Portugal) to c. 2300 BCE in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, c. 1800 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and as late as c. 1650 BCE in some areas; central Europe from c. 2500 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">inverted-bell-shaped beakers; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> daggers, V-perforated buttons and stone wrist-guards; early copper mining at Ross Island; gold lunulae; circular enclosures such as Pömmelte; the Amesbury Archer</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated graves, settlements, mines and monuments, radiocarbon dating, metal and isotope analyses, skeletal studies and ancient DNA (Olalde et al. 2018 and later); no writing</span></div>
        </div>
