    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Bell Beaker culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>About 4,800 years ago, people by the Tagus River in Portugal began making drinking cups shaped like an upside-down bell.</p><p>Archaeologists, people who dig up the past, call their way of life the Bell Beaker culture, after those cups.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Beaker groups travelled by sea and along rivers, reaching the Rhine, Britain, Ireland and Hungary.</p><p>Later many Beaker people moved to Britain, and within a few hundred years most people there were descended from them.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/copper.jpg" alt="Copper" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Copper <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jonathan Zander; derivative work by Materialscientist, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They were farmers who kept animals, grew barley and spun wool.</p><p>At Ross Island in Ireland they dug copper from the ground and made the first copper axes of Britain and Ireland.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Later they used tin from Cornwall, in Britain, to make a new metal called bronze.</p><p>They also made ornaments and round discs of gold.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>They often buried their dead one by one, curled up, with weapons beside them.</p><p>The Amesbury Archer, buried in the richest grave of his time in Britain, probably grew up near the Alps.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/knowth.jpg" alt="Knowth" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Knowth <span class="kid-tale-cr">Markiemcg1, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>When they came to Ireland, the old custom of building great stone tombs, like those at Knowth, came to an end.</p><p>Their beakers went out of use about 3,700 years ago, but their graves, mines and monuments remain.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Bell Beaker is an archaeologists’ label for a set of objects and customs, named after a drinking vessel shaped like an upside-down bell, that appears across western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from about 2800 BCE.</li><li>Beaker groups spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, gold and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>-bronze working along sea and river routes, from Ireland’s Ross Island mines to Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</li><li>Its early spread from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> seems to have involved little migration, but its later spread carried steppe-related ancestry that replaced about 90% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s gene pool.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Isotope analysis of the Amesbury Archer, the richest burial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> of his time (c. 2300 BCE), shows that he spent his childhood in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, probably in the region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

